  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14096328/assets/b7d9cf2ef89d1a21cd9062a9ef1e709fb1c98bfc7de75b6b0447a204bd8472fc.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            After some time, the duct's settings are changed. The diagram above shows the flow of air induced at the top of a room in the $xy$-plane around the duct's outlet. The velocity field $F$ in this plane is given by\n\n$$\nF=({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{i}-2xy\\,\\text{j}.\n$$\n\nFind the flux of the airflow across the boundary $C$ of the exit of the tube.\n\n*Hint: First check if $F$ is source-free.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required flux is $\\,{\u222e}_{C}^{}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that, if $F=P\\,\\text{i}+Q\\,\\text{j}$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D\u2286{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $\u2207\\cdot F=\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and end points, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $\u03c8(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, for our vector field $F=({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{i}-2xy\\,\\text{j}$, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202x}+\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(-2xy) \\\\  & =2x-2x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero. Hence,\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$",
